 17592186511175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511397 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511397 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511397 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186510887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511397 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9e0a4333-6056-5794-b8d1-b1934cbb02e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511415 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511415 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511415 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186510845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511415 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "be017598-1e24-5bf6-b57e-7e6e33872bae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511425 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511425 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511425 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511425 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511425 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186511215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511425 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511425 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511425 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186511182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511425 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cd25d2f7-fa41-5617-b9af-782c028c8030" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511460 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511460 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105709" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511460 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511460 #edb/ref 1071 "On which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-4x+4}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511460 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0a118e6f-2768-508a-b130-7e64bda76208" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511460 #edb/ref 1074 "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which makes the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have ${x}^{2}-4x+4=0,$ which corresponds to $x=2.$ \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=2.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-1,1]$ is allowed. The intervals $(-2,1]$ and $[-1,2)$ are not closed, while $[0,2]$ and $[-1,3]$ contain the point $x=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511465 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511465 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105670" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511465 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511465 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=(x-2)(x+1)^{2}.$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem **not** guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $[-2,2]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511465 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "14a3bc41-5c72-5f24-9c07-376f58252a5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511465 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,2],$ therefore, the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-2)$ and $f(2).$\n\nSince $f(-2)=-4$ and $f(2)=0,$ we must have \n$k\\in [-4,0].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=1$ does not belong to the interval $[-4,0].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511473 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511473 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105705" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511473 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511473 #edb/ref 1071 "On which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-9}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511473 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2715d4ad-4bbd-59ce-9226-02bf5ba9575d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511473 #edb/ref 1074 "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have ${x}^{2}-9=0,$ which corresponds to $x=-3$ or $x=3.$ \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the points $x=-3$ or $x=3.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-8,-4]$ is allowed. The intervals $(3,4)$ and $(-7,-3)$ are not closed, the interval $[-6,-1]$ contains the point $x=-3,$ while the interval $[3,5]$ contain the point $x=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511499 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511499 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105621" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511499 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511499 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/87/87bcea590bfb7357728d64cadabf4ab3e85135d312a60dd092ec546727a5fa30.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is defined and continuous on the closed interval $[-1,4].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\text{ ________ }$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c\\in [-1,4]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511499 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "60c91108-e44c-5a52-8d9f-b008dcd257b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511499 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,4],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-1)$ and $f(4)$ inclusively.\n\nSince $f(-1)=5$ and $f(4)=-1$, we must have \n$k\\in [-1,5].$\n\nTherefore, the missing part is \"$-1$ and $5$\".\n\n![](images/1c/1c823d5f99ac9a126d3144197a493f347df20215c30b5ee7b28dc136459f00a2.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511503 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511503 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48342" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511503 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511503 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-\\sqrt{x}$ which is defined and continuous on the closed interval $[0,4].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\text{ ________ }$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c\\in [0,4]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511503 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "649e731a-c63d-5be5-bf6d-a7054ad65b37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511503 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,4],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(0)$ and $f(4).$\n\nSince $f(0)=0$ and $f(4)=14$, we must have \n$k\\in [0,14].$\n\nTherefore, the missing part is \"$0$ and $14$\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511510 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511510 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344753" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511510 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511510 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [-3,4].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $-4$ | $3$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $-1$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=1?$\n\nThe equation $g(x)=1$ has at least\n{{field-1}} solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511510 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "75621f90-4584-51d0-a7ef-873c52eae724" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511510 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-3,4],$ we can apply the Intermediate Value Theorem (IVT) in each subinterval of $[-3,4].$\n\nWe will go through each subinterval of $[-3,4]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-3,-2],$ we have $g(-3)=-4$ and $g(-2)=3.$ Since $1\\in [-4,3],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-2,-1],$ we have $g(-2)=3$ and $g(-1)=0.$ Since $1\\in [0,3],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-1,1],$ we have $g(-1)=0$ and $g(1)=-2.$ Since $1\\notin [-2,0],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[1,3],$ we have $g(1)=-2$ and $g(3)=-5.$ Since $1\\notin [-5,-2],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[3,4],$ we have $g(3)=-5$ and $g(4)=-1.$ Since $1\\notin [-5,-1],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=1$ on the subintervals $[-3,-2]$ and $[-2,-1].$ \n\nTherefore, $g(x)=1$ has at least $2$ solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511518 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511518 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48425" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511518 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511518 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ which is defined and continuous on the closed interval $[0,8].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\text{ ________ }$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c\\in [0,8]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511518 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "889fc732-d136-5234-a89b-7831dcd5eacb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511518 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,8],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(0)$ and $f(8).$\n\nSince $f(0)=0$ and $f(8)=2$, we must have \n$k\\in [0,2].$\n\nTherefore, the missing part is \"$0$ and $2$\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511523 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511523 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47084" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511523 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511523 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=(x-1)(x+1)(x-2).$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem **not** guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $[0,3]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511523 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8c198801-a87e-5360-90a9-0643872362f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511523 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,3],$ therefore, the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(0)$ and $f(3).$\n\nSince $f(0)=2$ and $f(3)=8,$ we must have \n$k\\in [2,8].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=0$ does not belong to the interval $[2,8].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511534 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511534 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47053" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511534 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511534 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [-1,8].$ Some values of $f(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $5$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-5$ | $0$ | $4$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $8$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $f(x)=-2?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511534 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d898f3e-75a2-51d9-91a1-d58badf3f268" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511534 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f$ is a continuous function on $[-1,8]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-1,8].$\n\nWe will go through each subinterval of $[-1,8]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-1,0],$ we have $f(-1)=-5$ and $f(0)=0.$ Since $-2\\in [-5,0],$ the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[0,2],$ we have $f(0)=0$ and $f(2)=4.$ Since $-2\\notin [0,4],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[2,4],$ we have $f(2)=4$ and $f(4)=-1.$ Since $-2\\notin [-1,4],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[4,5],$ we have $f(4)=-1$ and $f(5)=-4.$ Since $-2\\in [-4,-1],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[5,8],$ we have $f(5)=-4$ and $f(8)=8.$ Since $-2\\in [-4,8],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $f(x)=-2$ on the subintervals $[-1,0],$ $[4,5],$ and $[5,8].$ Therefore, $f(x)=-2$ has at least three solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511583 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511583 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48358" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511583 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511583 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/de/dededc3b9924ff278f45a6149a0b7a5ca0464ee2c3b80aa85d12187a86a93725.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is defined and continuous on the closed interval $[-2,3].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $-1$ and $1$ inclusively, then there exists a number $\\text{ ________ }$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511583 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fc22bd7d-9c0b-50f8-80e8-b40ec1e8460f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511583 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,3]$, the intermediate value theorem states that \n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $f(-2)$ and $f(3)$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c\\in [-2,3]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nFrom the graph we see that $f(-2)=1$ and $f(3)=-1.$Therefore, the missing part is \"$c\\in [-2,3]$\"\n\n![](images/85/85a590ba283dae96329692fee1aa6e08045fb06e5baa8a42d8308fe8c2558f02.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
